{
	title: 'Web push: notifications on a phone with the web app on its home screen',
	spec: 'The web client is an installable web app and can get push notifications, including on an iPhone that added it to its home screen (iOS 16.4+; Safari tabs get no push, only home-screen apps do). Pieces, stdlib and WebCrypto only: the host serves a manifest (name Hal, display standalone, icons) and a service worker (/sw.js) whose push handler shows the notification and whose notificationclick opens or focuses the session\'s page. A "Notify me" control in the web client (shown only when the Push API exists and permission is not yet granted; the permission prompt must come from that tap, never on load) subscribes with the host\'s VAPID public key and sends the subscription to the host over the WebSocket. The host keeps a P-256 VAPID key pair in state/ (made once, 0600) and subscriptions per browser in state/, drops one when the push service answers 404 or 410, and sends a push (RFC 8030 POST to the subscription endpoint, VAPID JWT ES256 per RFC 8292, payload encrypted aes128gcm per RFC 8291, TTL a few hours) when a session needs the user: a turn completed or failed, a question or approval waits. It skips the push when some client has that session focused and visible (clients report visibility), so a user at the screen is not buzzed. The payload is small: session name or id and one line (e.g. "done", "asks: …"), never tool output or secrets. The app icon badge counts sessions needing attention (navigator.setAppBadge) where supported. Pushes need HTTPS, so they work through the webUrl deployment (HTTPS reverse proxy), not http://localhost. A setting push (default true) turns sending off. The web menu\'s Notifications dialog manages push: this device\'s state (unavailable outside an HTTPS home-screen app, blocked in Settings, on, off) with Turn on (the tap that asks permission) and Turn off (unsubscribes and makes the host forget the endpoint); Send test, which pushes to this device at once, even while it watches, and reports the push service\'s answer; and every registered device with the browser\'s own label (e.g. \'iPhone · Home Screen app\', at most 80 characters), its date added and a Remove button. The host stores label and date with the subscription and answers push commands with the device list. Tests: VAPID JWT verifies with the public key; payload encryption decrypts with a test subscription\'s keys (RFC 8291 test vector); a 410 drops the subscription; no push while a client has the session visible; a push after a completed turn when none has.',
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'RFC 8291 §3/§4, §5 and Appendix A: each send generates a separate ephemeral P-256 ECDH key and 16-byte salt; HKDF-SHA256 with salt=auth secret, info="WebPush: info" + NUL + receiver 65-byte point + sender 65-byte point yields 32-byte IKM; second HKDF with random salt derives 16-byte CEK from "Content-Encoding: aes128gcm" + NUL and 12-byte nonce from "Content-Encoding: nonce" + NUL. AES-GCM plaintext ends with 0x02, one record, 16-byte tag. Body = 16-byte salt + 4-byte big-endian rs (4096) + 1-byte key length (65) + 65-byte ephemeral uncompressed point + ciphertext. Total <=4096 bytes (plaintext <=3993). RFC test vector in push-crypto.test.ts; base64url-decode vector header and ciphertext separately since string concatenation at non-3-byte-aligned 86-byte boundary is wrong.',
		'RFC 8292: VAPID is a persistent P-256 ECDSA signing key, DIFFERENT from ephemeral ECDH key. Authorization: vapid t=<base64url JWT header>.<claims>.<64-byte raw ES256 signature>, k=<base64url 65-byte uncompressed public point>; aud is push ENDPOINT origin (not example.com), exp <=24h, sub can be https://example.com. Apple requires the same public VAPID key used in PushManager.subscribe. Apple recommends no more than hourly JWT refresh.',
		'Apple web push: iOS 16.4+ only installed Home Screen web apps (macOS Safari 16+ supports tabs); HTTPS via example.com, manifest display standalone and SW required. Call subscribe directly on user tap (not startup), show notification immediately on every push or Safari revokes permission; permission is not for silent pushes. Apple supports Badging API; TTL header must be positive; push.apple.com accepts HTTP/1.1 or HTTP/2; 410 means expired subscription. Ref: https://developer.apple.com/documentation/usernotifications/sending-web-push-notifications-in-web-apps-and-browsers.md',
		'Implementation uses state/push-vapid.ason and state/push-subscriptions.ason via liveFiles.liveFile(mode 0600), never reads private key into HTML; page embeds only base64url uncompressed public point so the subscribe tap can use it without a network fetch. /manifest.webmanifest, /sw.js and PNG icons are unauthenticated to allow installation/background push, but subscription registration is via authenticated same-origin WebSocket. Browser page reports focus+visibility per shown tab; host suppresses only a matching visible connection. Browser startup resends an existing subscription without re-prompting; no foreground polling. Badge follows shared tab attention, which hidden pages do not clear.',
		'Notify me appears after the HTTPS page registers /sw.js; on tap PushManager.subscribe is called synchronously before any await (iOS user gesture restriction). The host sends only a sanitized {id,title,body} payload; the worker validates id before opening/focusing same-origin session URL. VAPID JWT is reused per push-service origin for 11 hours (12-hour expiry) as Apple warns against refreshing more than hourly. Test on iPhone: add https://example.com to Home Screen via Safari Share, open that icon, log in with ./run auth code, tap Notify me and allow, background the app, then complete a turn in Hal with no visible client focused on the session; tap notification to return to it. Safari tabs alone have no iOS push.',
		"Users could not see, test or remove devices: the only control was a Notify button that vanished once permission was granted, so a dead iPhone subscription was invisible. The device dialog lives beside task py's planned notification history; keep them separate sections.",
		'Invariant: the dialog shows Turn on whenever the host does not list this device, and names why push is unavailable. A device removed from the list kept its browser subscription, so the dialog said On with no way back; a failed service worker start was swallowed. Pushes and skipped pushes are logged to diag.log.'
	],
	needs: ['8a']
}
